<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  FastDDS profile — SHM transport only.

  FastDDS auto-discovers SHM as a same-host transport on Humble
  defaults; this profile makes the choice explicit by registering an
  SHM transport descriptor and registering the SHM transport descriptor beside the (required) built-in transports
  (which would also enable UDPv4 as a fallback).

  segment_size = 64 MiB. Empirically tested 256 MiB and it BROKE
  even size=64 on jazzy (deterministic latency-node bootstrap hang).
  64 MiB was working for sizes ≤1 MB; 16 MiB payload at jazzy/kilted
  needs different tuning (e.g. flow_controllers, asynchronous mode)
  not segment_size. Don't naively bump segment_size without re-
  verifying small payloads still work. port_queue_capacity stays at
  the original 1024 for the same reason.

  ## Why no DataSharing AUTOMATIC block here — this is the
  ## STOCK-DEFAULTS lane (audit fix C1)

  Under ROS 2, DataSharing (Fast DDS's true zero-copy path) is OFF
  by default on EVERY distro in the matrix, regardless of Fast DDS's
  own AUTO default: rmw_fastrtps calls writer_qos.data_sharing().off()
  and reader_qos.data_sharing().off() inside
  `if (!participant_info->leave_middleware_default_qos)` on both the
  jazzy and lyrical/rolling branches (rmw_fastrtps_cpp
  publisher.cpp / subscription.cpp), and leave_middleware_default_qos
  flips only with RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1. So this profile —
  run WITHOUT that env var (run_bench.sh unsets it for the shm lane)
  — measures Fast DDS exactly as an out-of-box ROS 2 user gets it:
  SHM *transport* engaged, DataSharing off. The vendor-recommended
  zero-copy configuration is a separate labeled lane: shm mode `zc`
  (configs/fastdds_zc.xml + RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML=1, the
  rmw_fastrtps README's own recipe).

  Two claims an earlier revision of this comment made are FALSE and
  are corrected here so the suite doesn't publish wrong statements
  about the compared stack's behavior:
    - PR ros2/rmw_fastrtps#568 (merged 2023-01, Iron+) decoupled
      LOANED MESSAGES from data-sharing (can_loan_messages = is_plain
      since Iron). It did NOT remove the data_sharing().off()
      overrides — those persist through jazzy AND lyrical. So on
      Iron+ what engages automatically with a plain type is LOANS
      (the API-boundary borrow/take), NOT DataSharing; a `loaned=1`
      column on a stock fastdds cell does not prove a zero-copy data
      path.
    - Reliability never gates rmw_fastrtps loans in any era, and
      RELIABLE-vs-BEST_EFFORT is not in Fast DDS's documented
      data-sharing constraint list either (bounded plain non-keyed
      type, PREALLOCATED* memory policy, reader depth <= writer
      depth).

  Humble empirical note (why the zc lane is jazzy/lyrical territory):
  Humble's apt Fast-DDS 2.6.x segfaulted parsing the data_sharing XML
  elements (a one-shot `ros2 topic pub` exits rc=139, measured in the
  May-2026 campaign) — a humble zc cell fails its verify probe
  loudly; humble stays stock-defaults-only, as a measured limitation.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
        <type>SHM</type>
        <segment_size>67108864</segment_size>
        <port_queue_capacity>1024</port_queue_capacity>
        <healthy_check_timeout_ms>1000</healthy_check_timeout_ms>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>
    <participant profile_name="participant_default" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>shm_transport</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- useBuiltinTransports=true is REQUIRED on Fast DDS 2.14+/3.x
             (jazzy / kilted): PDP discovery always uses UDP/TCP even
             when SHM is enabled (Fast DDS 3.x SHM transport docs +
             Transport FAQ). Disabling builtin transports kills
             participant matching, manifesting as "Waiting for at least
             1 matching subscription(s)" → context invalid. Our SHM
             user transport descriptor still tunes the SHM segment;
             intra-host DataSharing prefers SHM over UDP automatically. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>true</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
